 Hear me out: printf '\033[38;2;0;255;0m GREEN TEXT \033[0m\n' prints GREEN TEXT in the color green to the terminal. The current step is to get this to work via python. This sequence was given to me by an LLM, so I don't know, maybe a 2/10 cognitive deficit?
 That is, I would follow the format specified on https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/console/console-virtual-terminal-sequences#extended-colors. It's simple: the first character is the escape sequence (\033), the second one is called "control sequence introducer" as mentioned on https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ANSI_escape_code#SGR_(Select_Graphic_Rendition)_parame, that is [. It starts a sequence. The sequence itself is simple too - it's a select graphici rendition (SDR) command:
 38;2;R;G;Bm sets RGB to foreground and 48;2;R;G;Bm sets RGB to the background. Finally, we clear the color styling back to default using ESC[0m which is \033[0m. Without resetting the command is even simpler: printf '\033[38;2;0;255;0m GREEN TEXT\n'. Now the question is - how to implement this in python?
